Title: The Velveteen Rabbit
Credit: Written by
Author: Margery Williams
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = stylized children's picture-book illustration; softly painted storybook textures, gentle natural light, and consistent illustrated humans, toys, animals, and magic.

FADE IN:

INT. NURSERY - CHRISTMAS MORNING

A fat, bunchy VELVETEEN RABBIT with spotted brown-and-white velveteen, pink sateen ears, thread whiskers, and a holly sprig sits in the BOY'S stocking.

(SOUND: paper rustles, a clockwork mouse clicks, distant family laughter)

The BOY, a small child in a white nightshirt with tousled brown hair, lifts the Rabbit from the stocking and hugs him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Here was once a velveteen rabbit, and in the beginning he was really splendid.

The Boy sets the Rabbit amid nuts, oranges, a toy engine, and chocolate almonds.

(SOUND: toy engine wheels rattle across the floor)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For at least two hours the Boy loved him.

Aunts and uncles flood the nursery doorway with parcels. The Boy turns toward new gifts, leaving the Rabbit on the floor.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the excitement of looking at all the new presents, the Velveteen Rabbit was forgotten.

INT. NURSERY - NIGHT

Moonlight falls across a toy cupboard and nursery fender. The Rabbit lies beside the SKIN HORSE, an old brown toy horse with bald patches, exposed seams, and a thin, pulled-out tail.

(SOUND: fire crackles softly in the fender)

Mechanical toys stand stiffly nearby. A MODEL BOAT, paint chipped along its hull, points its tiny rigging toward the ceiling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The mechanical toys were very superior, and looked down upon every one else.

The Rabbit turns toward the Skin Horse.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
What is REAL? Does it mean having things that buzz inside you and a stick-out handle?

SKIN HORSE
Real isn't how you are made. It's a thing that happens to you.

The Skin Horse's stitched smile holds steady.

SKIN HORSE
When a child loves you for a long, long time, not just to play with, but REALLY loves you, then you become Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it hurt?

SKIN HORSE
Sometimes. When you are Real you don't mind being hurt.

(SOUND: a nursery clock ticks)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Does it happen all at once, like being wound up, or bit by bit?

SKIN HORSE
It doesn't happen all at once. You become. It takes a long time.

The Rabbit studies his own bright thread whiskers.

SKIN HORSE
By the time you are Real, most of your hair has been loved off, and you get very shabby.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I suppose you are real?

SKIN HORSE
The Boy's Uncle made me Real. Once you are Real you can't become unreal again. It lasts for always.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

NANA, a brisk woman in a dark dress and white apron, searches the bed for a missing china dog. She grabs the Rabbit by one ear from the open toy cupboard.

(SOUND: bedclothes swish and a door latch clicks)

NANA
Here, take your old Bunny! He'll do to sleep with you!

She puts the Rabbit into the Boy's arms. The Boy buries his face in the Rabbit's soft velveteen.

The Boy makes a tunnel beneath the bedclothes and pushes the Rabbit through it.

BOY
This is like the burrows the real rabbits live in.

(SOUND: the night-light hums; the Boy whispers beneath the covers)

The Boy falls asleep with both hands clasped around the Rabbit. Night after night, the Rabbit's pink nose grows rubbed bare, his tail loosens, and his coat becomes shabby.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The little Rabbit was very happy that he never noticed how his beautiful velveteen fur was getting shabbier and shabbier.

EXT. GARDEN - DUSK

The Boy pushes the Rabbit in a wheelbarrow past flower beds. The Rabbit, now faded and dusty, sits among grass clippings beneath raspberry canes.

(SOUND: wheelbarrow wheel squeaks; bees drone among flowers)

The Boy builds a tiny fairy hut around him. A call from the house sends the Boy running away.

Later, darkness settles. NANA crosses the wet lawn carrying a candle and finds the Rabbit soaked with dew.

(SOUND: night insects chirr; candle flame sputters)

NANA
You must have your old Bunny! Fancy all that fuss for a toy!

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

The wet Rabbit rests on the bed. The Boy, in his nightshirt, sits upright and stretches both hands toward him.

(SOUND: rain taps gently against the window)

BOY
Give me my Bunny! You mustn't say that. He isn't a toy. He's REAL!

Nana pauses with the Rabbit in her hands. She looks at his worn face and boot-button eyes.

NARRATOR (V.O)
When the little Rabbit heard that, he was happy, for he knew that what the Skin Horse had said was true at last.

The Boy kisses the Rabbit's faded nose and sleeps with him beneath his chin.

(SOUND: a quiet lullaby on the nursery music box)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The nursery magic had happened to him.

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SUMMER EVENING

The Boy makes a nest for the Rabbit among tall bracken, then runs away to pick flowers. Ants cross the Rabbit's faded velvet paws.

(SOUND: leaves whisper; distant children play)

Two WILD RABBITS, sleek and brown with bright eyes and long whiskers, creep from the bracken. They twitch their noses at the shabby toy.

WILD RABBIT
Why don't you get up and play with us?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't feel like it.

One Wild Rabbit hops sideways and rises on his hind legs.

WILD RABBIT
Can you hop on your hind legs?

The Rabbit remains still, his back one soft pincushion shape.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't want to.

WILD RABBIT
He hasn't got any hind legs! Fancy a rabbit without any hind legs!

(SOUND: the wild rabbits thump and rustle through the fern)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I have got hind legs! I am sitting on them!

The Wild Rabbit dances in a circle.

WILD RABBIT
Then stretch them out and show me, like this!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I don't like dancing. I'd rather sit still.

The Wild Rabbit's whiskers brush the Rabbit's ear. He jerks backward.

WILD RABBIT
He doesn't smell right! He isn't a rabbit at all! He isn't real!

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am Real! The Boy said so!

Footsteps approach. The Boy runs through the bracken. The Wild Rabbits flash away, white tails vanishing between the trees.

(SOUND: the Boy's feet pound closer; bracken snaps back into place)

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Come back and play with me! Oh, do come back! I know I am Real!

The Rabbit lies alone as white moths flutter above him.

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Weeks later, the Rabbit is threadbare and loose, his brown spots faded, pink ears grey, and whiskers almost entirely loved away. The Boy holds him against a flushed cheek.

(SOUND: a feverish cough; a bedside clock ticks through the night)

The Boy tosses under blankets, burning with scarlet fever. A DOCTOR in a black coat examines him while Nana tends the lamp.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Strange people came and went in the nursery, and a light burned all night.

The Rabbit remains hidden under the bedclothes, pressed close against the Boy.

(SOUND: spoon clinks against a medicine glass)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He never stirred, for he was afraid that if they found him some one might take him away.

The fever breaks. Days later, the Boy sits up with a picture book while the Rabbit leans against his side.

(SOUND: birds sing through the open window)

INT. BOY'S BEDROOM - MORNING

Sunlight pours through wide-open windows. The Boy, pale but recovering, sits wrapped in a shawl on the balcony threshold. The shabby Rabbit peeps from tangled bedclothes.

(SOUND: sparrows chatter outside; a carriage rolls faintly down the lane)

The Doctor speaks to Nana beside a pile of books and toys.

DOCTOR
The room is to be disinfected. All the books and toys that he played with in bed must be burnt.

The Rabbit's button eyes face the bright window.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hurrah! To-morrow we shall go to the seaside!

Nana spots the Rabbit.

NANA
How about his old Bunny?

DOCTOR
That? Why, it's a mass of scarlet fever germs! Burn it at once.

NANA
What? Nonsense! Get him a new one.

The Boy is carried away toward the waiting carriage, never seeing Nana lift the Rabbit with the old picture books.

(SOUND: carriage wheels begin to roll; the bedroom door shuts)

EXT. GARDEN BEHIND THE FOWL-HOUSE - NIGHT

The Rabbit lies in an untied sack with old picture books and rubbish. Beyond him, raspberry canes rise dark and close beneath the moon.

(SOUND: hens shift in their coop; cold wind stirs the sack)

The Rabbit wriggles his head through the opening. His thin, threadbare coat trembles in the night air.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
That night the Boy slept in a different bedroom, and he had a new bunny to sleep with him.

The Rabbit looks toward the garden where fairy huts once stood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Of what use was it to be loved and lose one's beauty and become Real if it all ended like this?

A real tear slides down his shabby velvet nose and drops into the soil.

(SOUND: the tear lands softly; the wind falls still)

A green shoot pushes up where the tear fell. It opens into a flower with emerald leaves and a golden cup.

(SOUND: petals unfurl with a faint crystalline chime)

The NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY steps from the blossom. She wears pearl and dew-drop garments, flowers in her hair, and carries a gentle golden light.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Little Rabbit, don't you know who I am?

VELVETEEN RABBIT
I am afraid I don't.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I am the nursery magic Fairy. I take care of all the playthings that the children have loved.

She gathers the Rabbit into her arms and kisses his damp, faded nose.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
When they are old and worn out, and the children don't need them any more, then I take them away and turn them into Real.

VELVETEEN RABBIT
Wasn't I Real before?

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
You were Real to the Boy, because he loved you. Now you shall be Real to every one.

EXT. WOODLAND GLADE - MOONLIT NIGHT

The Fairy carries the Rabbit into a silvered glade. Wild Rabbits dance on velvet grass, then stop in a ring around her.

(SOUND: rabbits thump softly; moonlit leaves shiver overhead)

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
I've brought you a new playfellow. You must be very kind to him and teach him all he needs to know in Rabbit-land.

The Fairy sets the Rabbit on the grass and kisses him once more.

NURSERY MAGIC FAIRY
Run and play, little Rabbit!

The Rabbit sits still, staring at the Wild Rabbits. Something tickles his nose.

(SOUND: a tiny sneeze; the rabbits' paws patter in the grass)

He lifts a hind toe to scratch. He freezes.

Brown fur, soft and shiny, covers him. His ears twitch; long whiskers brush the grass. Four living legs gather beneath him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Instead of dingy velveteen he had brown fur, soft and shiny.

The Rabbit leaps. Then he springs sideways, whirls, and races among the other rabbits.

(SOUND: rapid paws drum the turf; bright woodland music rises)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He was a Real Rabbit at last, at home with the other rabbits.

EXT. WOODLAND BRACKEN - SPRING DAY

Autumn and winter have passed. The Boy, stronger now, plays among warm spring bracken. He wears a blue coat and has the same tousled brown hair.

(SOUND: spring birdsong; branches creak in a mild breeze)

Two rabbits creep from the ferns. One is brown all over. The other is brown, with faint old spotted markings showing beneath his fur.

The marked Rabbit watches the Boy with round black eyes.

The Boy kneels, studying him.

BOY
Why, he looks just like my old Bunny that was lost when I had scarlet fever!

The Rabbit remains still for one tender moment, then turns toward the sunlit wood.

(SOUND: grass stirs beneath the rabbits' paws)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But he never knew that it really was his own Bunny, come back to look at the child who had first helped him to be Real.

The Rabbit runs with the other rabbits into the bracken.

FADE OUT.

THE END
